      "level": 1,
      "content": "Introduction to Arrays"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We're all familiar with variables, which are used to store individual pieces of information. For instance:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "A single score can be stored in a variable like score",
        "A single day's temperature can be stored in temperature."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "However, what happens when there are 50 students or 7 days? Creating one variable for each piece of information—like score1, score2, ..., score50—becomes inefficient, error-prone, and hard to manage."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For such situations, we need to use a collection of data, known as an array. For example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "To store a student's scores in five subjects, we can use an array to keep all the data together in one organized group."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Array is a collection of related items",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "To record temperatures for a week, we can use an array to store all the daily readings together in one organized group."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now that you know why arrays are important, let's learn how to create an array next."
    },
